\title{Formal Verification in Proof Assistants}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2025-09-02}
\meta{toc}{true}

\p{
    Speaking more broadly, [formal verification](dhsorens-000X) in [proof assistants](dhsorens-000B) is interesting because in the robustly mathematical setting provided by proof assistants, one can state and prove arbitrary properties—in other words, almost \em{anything}.

    In some ways, this is a blessing and a curse.
    Much like with programming languages, being able to state and do arbitrary things is not always an advantage.
    Formal counterparts of informal specifications can vary widely, without an obvious way to compare them or evaluate them for correctness (see the Introduction of my [PhD thesis](docs/sorensen-phd-thesis.pdf)).

}

\p{
    In my [PhD](dhsorens-phd) and subsequent writings (e.g. [this paper](dhsorens-000L) or my [research statement](docs/sorensen-research-statement.pdf)), I have argued that the robustly mathematical setting of proof assistants offers us an opportunity for unmatched rigor, but we must draw on mathematical practice and tradition and use them in a more sophisticated way than we are using them now.
}